        "title": "Revising for exams",
        "text": "The way to do well in History is to know which study techniques work best for you as an individual. Even so, here are some sensible guidelines that are worth following.\n\nA  Use good study skills from the beginning of your course, rather than looking for magical solutions a few weeks before the exam. Ideally, every evening you should read through the notes you made that day, improving them and making sure they are useful. Then, every few months, go through all your notes — this will make your final revision much easier. In this way, essential information will be stored in your long-term memory and will be easy to recall, even under stressful exam conditions. You will also avoid last-minute cramming, which is seldom useful.\n\nB  Make sure that you have a copy of the syllabus or course handbook. Check the format of your exam. How many papers are there? How many questions must be answered? Are there any compulsory sections? Sort out any outside or personal problems that might hold back your progress; if necessary, talk to your tutor, the student counselling service or a doctor. From Easter, give up or cut down your weekend job until after the exams.\n\nC  Listen to your teacher's advice on important areas or likely questions. Choose topics for revision. Decide how many you need to know about: for example, if you have to answer four questions, go through the papers of the last few years and make sure that you could answer five or six of them. If you can answer all of them, be careful — you are probably working too hard.\n\nD  Do not work from poor material. Improve your notes by comparing them with a friend's or by reading them alongside a textbook, and make any additions and changes that are needed. Make sure that you understand your notes before you try to memorise them — if you don't, the ideas simply will not stick. Underline, colour or highlight headings and key points.\n\nE  The more often your notes are rewritten, the better you will remember them. Summarise the key information on each topic on one A4 page. Then shorten it again onto small index cards: carry them around with you and learn them whenever you have a few spare minutes. If you find it hard to remember key quotations or dates, write them out and put them in places around the house where you will see them often. You could also record them. But remember to think actively about key issues as well as memorising information. Your aim should be to look at old, familiar material in a new way.\n\nF  Working with a group (the right group for you) will let you share ideas, notes and books, and can help to reduce boredom and stress. Revising in pairs is good, but working in groups of three or four is better. The ideal is to meet for sessions of two or three hours, two or three times a week, at home, school or college — look for a place to work with as few distractions as possible. Discuss questions or problems, do timed questions, read out answers for the group to criticise, test each other and prepare outline answers.\n\nG  When your teachers give you practice exams to do in class, take them seriously and do your best. They will help you to assess your progress and get used to working under strict exam conditions. Afterwards, take note of the feedback you get. Identify the mistakes you made. Did you include too little information, misread the questions or run out of time? What does your mark tell you about your revision techniques?\n\nH  Far more exams are failed because of too little work than too much. But the brightest students often work too hard at revision and worry unnecessarily. So take regular exercise, get plenty of sleep and keep up a sensible social life. If you are a terrible worrier, then by all means carry on with gentle revision until the last moment: you can't worry if your mind is busy with something else. But remember that the aim is to reach your peak at the right time, so make sure you do not go into the exam room exhausted from overwork. Frantic late-night cramming can easily be avoided by using the revision techniques described above."
